Z||000000||JohnsFiero||08-25-2000||12:01 PM||||Hi - kind of new to the forum so bear with me  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Have an '84SE where the previous owner disabled the blue dingy but instead of disconnecting the box appears to have disconnected the wire.  I found a connector with an orange and brown (might be gray - it was dark last night) wire behind the radio.  Is this what I am looking for?  If so, where does it go?  If not, where does it go?  My manuals seem to be of no help on this.  Hazards and everything else seems to work fine.  Need to get this fixed so the wife will stop driving with the emergency brake on  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">  Thanks - John||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phil||08-25-2000||04:24 PM||||Most likely your radio loses the presets and the clock doesn't work either. The orange and gray wires go into the radio.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phil||08-25-2000||04:25 PM||||Most likely your radio loses the presets and the clock doesn't work either. The orange and gray wires go into the radio.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phil||08-25-2000||04:26 PM||||Blast a double||||reg||1
Z||000004||286SE'S||08-25-2000||05:57 PM||||Pull the "blue dingy thingy" out of its socket and see if all 8 pins are still there. A common "fix" was to cut off the<BR>power tab to shut it up. It may also just be a dead one, very common. Have you tried another unit? Some of the red ones from a Buick or Olds will work but they don't have an input for the brake chime, which isn't what you want. You may want to look at the back of the socket, it is easly removed from its bracket. Some people here hate that chime unit but I found that they all do not sound the same and went through 3 of them before I found one that had a nice deep tone, most were harsh beeps that I hated too.<BR>Terry<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||08-26-2000||09:32 PM||||does the brake warning light come on in the dash when the handle is pulled? If not the problem is on that end not the blue box.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||JohnsFiero||08-27-2000||12:45 AM||||Yep - brake light in the dash still works.  Pulled the box and everything seems to check out.  It works in my other car.  Just not this one.  Any other ideas?<P>Radio works and keeps the presets with no problems.||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||08-27-2000||02:05 PM||||take out the one screw that holds the socket to its bracket on the heater box and see if any wires are cut.||||reg||1
Z||000008||mdjoneses||08-27-2000||02:35 PM||||Chime Units for the Pontiac Fiero <P><BR>--------------------------------------------------------------------------------<P>The Chime Unit is located under the passengers side dash. It is a blue box beside a flasher unit.  <BR>The Chime Socket looking at the base from under the dash: <BR>This side is most distant from flasher & fuse on the same base. <P>Terminal A will be the top left hand terminal if you are holding the chime in your hands and looking at the pins. <P>Note:<BR>Your chime unit might have the letters A-G on it, it might have 1-8, and again it might not have anything! When cutting wires, use the color coding and not the pin location since this may differ between model years. <P>Chime codes: <P>Fast pulsed chime, parking brake warning. <BR>Fast pulsed chime, lights on. <BR>Fast chime, key warning. <BR>Slow chime, seatbelt warning.<P>DO NOT cut the following wires or traces. They are necessary for the chime to operate: <P>A Pink/Black wire goes to pin A. This is the ignition input. <BR>An Orange wire goes to pin B. This is the power input. <BR>A Black wire goes to pin C. This is the ground. <BR>A Yellow wire goes to pin G. This is the "Fasten Seat Belt" OUTPUT wire that turns on the instrument cluster light. <BR>Cut the input wire or circuit board trace to stop the chime for these sensors: <P>(Another) Black wire goes to pin D. This is the sensor line for the seatbelt warning. <BR>A tan/white wire goes to pin E. This is the sensor line for the parking brake warning. <BR>A light green wire goes to pin F. This is the "Key in Ignition Input". <BR>The gray wire goes to pin G. This is the "Lights-on Input". <P>--------------------------------------------------------------------------------<P>Return to the Michigan Fiero Club Page<BR>This page is maintained by the Michigan Fiero Club<BR>||||reg||2
Z||000009||StuGood||08-27-2000||06:18 PM||||Quickest fix:<P>If the brake warning light in the dash still works, maybe you can train your wife to scan the dash lights.  Might be nice if she were on the lookout for idiot light action, too?  ||||reg||12
Z||000010||fiero56||08-27-2000||06:59 PM||||Wait a minute, you're saying there is a seatbelt chime? Mine doesn't do that?||||reg||1
Z||000011||mdjoneses||08-27-2000||10:26 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.michiganfieroclub.org/chime.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.michiganfieroclub.org/chime.htm</A>   This site has a lot of good fix it instructions. It says there is a seat belt chime. I think I hear it every time I go to start my car only for a few seconds. I bet if I got in and put the belt on first and turned the key I would hear nothing. I'll try that next time.   Mark86se2.8  P.S. My wife doesn't drive a standard tranny--thats why I bought it!!!||||reg||2
Z||000012||theogre||08-28-2000||10:29 AM||||Problem is the Michigan site's chime list is wrong. I just was working on mine this weekend. I don't know how they got that one but it's definatly messed up. Maybe it's from an 84 but I don't think those are wired any dif. <P>I just posted some notes on this to the cave. (Link at bottom of page.) there's a link for a good wire list in that article. I'm trying to solve the Random Tone problem so many of us, including me, have.<P>Yes, there's a seat belt chime. I think it only rings if you don't have your belt on when you start the car. It times out after a few seconds. I haven't played with that one much.||||reg||1
Z||000013||theogre||08-29-2000||08:33 PM||||I found yet another problem with this. I'll look at mine more tomorow.<P>If you think the box is dead or it's makeing ratty sounding tones then take down the socket and check the terminal with 2 orange wires. wiggle pull and push on the wires. Try to hold the box tight to the socket when you do this. You don's want the box to move in the socket.<P>If the box works when the wire gets a good bite then this terminal is scragged. <P>I ran into this tonight as I worked on figureing out random tones. This wire alone could be causing the tones. It's the main power feed. I'll work in it more over the next few days and see if it solves the random tones too.<P>((The other main culprit for random tone looks like it is the wire to the hand brake lever. It may be getting pinched under the seat rail.))||||reg||1
Z||000014||JohnsFiero||08-29-2000||09:35 PM||||Ogre - I've followed everything you've said and still can't find the problem.  How difficult would it be to trace the wires back.  Seems like the blue box isn't getting any power.  If you were to cut the wire, where would be the "best" place to do it?  This is really annoying - about to say screw it and pretend like its that way on purpose.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000015||theogre||08-29-2000||11:41 PM||||There is no place but near the box really that you'd cut it. No-one's likely to cut power to it. it's easier just to pull the box. The box's power wire goes to things besides the box. you have to cut it at the box then splice the 2 wires so other stuff won't crap out. (Most people don't know how to release these terminals so that would also be unlikely.)<P>get the wire list from the link in my cave.<P>put the parking lights on with the key off then wriggle the chime. Did it sound at all, even really weak? Yes? then one or more terminals are hosed in the socket.<P>No? put a meter or test light on the orange wired terminal (terminal B)and see if there's power. You can get a small meter cheap at radio shack. maybe even the tool area at Wall/K mart, etc.<P>The problem with the terminal and the orange wires is that the terminal is made to hold 1 wire and GM stuffed 2 in it. What can happen when you do that is the wire breaks, often inside the insulation, and it can be a real pain.<P>If there's no power on the terminal stick a sewing pin into each wire and test it. if the pin shows voltage then the wire broke in the insulation at the terminal.<P>Next check the main ground. that's the black wire next to the orange. (terminal G) It should be 0 or nearly 0 ohms to the car frame.||||reg||1
